
Your current README specifies `yolov8n.onnx` and attempts to download it during setup.  However, that old URL in the README is no longer a reliable download source; a current ONNX copy is available from Hugging Face. ([Hugging Face][1])

### Download it directly

From your project root:

```bash
mkdir -p models

wget -O models/yolov8n.onnx \
https://huggingface.co/webml/yolov8n/resolve/main/onnx/yolov8n.onnx
```

Then verify:

```bash
ls -lh models/yolov8n.onnx
```

You should see a file around **12.8 MB**. ([Hugging Face][1])

Your project should then contain:

```text
orange-pi-ai-cctv/
│
├── models/
│   └── yolov8n.onnx
│
├── src/
│   ├── main.c
│   ├── detector.c
│   ├── rtsp_capture.c
│   └── ...
│
└── Makefile
```

### Important for your C-only conversion

The `.onnx` file itself **does not contain Python code**. It is the neural-network model.

So our C application can eventually do:

```text
RTSP Camera
     ↓
Frame
     ↓
OpenCV / FFmpeg
     ↓
YOLOv8n ONNX
     ↓
ONNX Runtime C API
     ↓
Detection Results
     ↓
Tracking / Database / Alert
```

Ultralytics documents ONNX as a deployment format, and for C/C++ applications the model can be executed through a native runtime such as ONNX Runtime. ([Ultralytics Docs][2])

**One correction to the previous plan:** because you specifically want **100% C**, we should use the **ONNX Runtime C API**, not Python `onnxruntime` or the Python Ultralytics package.

If you want, the next step can be **`detector.c` that loads `models/yolov8n.onnx` and performs YOLOv8 inference in pure C**.

[1]: https://huggingface.co/webml/yolov8n/blob/main/onnx/yolov8n.onnx?utm_source=chatgpt.com "onnx/yolov8n.onnx · webml/yolov8n at main"
[2]: https://docs.ultralytics.com/modes/export?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Model Export with Ultralytics YOLO | Ultralytics"


Important point about AI

There is one major issue we should handle carefully.

Your current project uses YOLO/ONNX Runtime, OpenCV, face recognition, ANPR, DeepSORT, PostgreSQL, Redis and Telegram. The README currently installs Python packages such as opencv-python, onnxruntime, ultralytics, requests, etc.

We cannot simply translate Python syntax line-by-line into C.

Instead, we need to replace each Python dependency with an appropriate C/C-compatible API/library.

For example:

Python
   │
   ├── OpenCV Python
   ├── ONNX Runtime Python
   ├── PostgreSQL Python
   ├── Redis Python
   ├── Requests
   └── Telegram Python
          │
          ▼
       C/C API

And the final runtime should become:

                 ┌─────────────────┐
                 │     main.c      │
                 │ System Manager  │
                 └────────┬────────┘
                          │
        ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
        ▼                 ▼                 ▼
   RTSP Camera       AI Detection       Motion
        │                 │                 │
        ▼                 ▼                 ▼
   Frame Buffer       YOLO/ONNX        Motion Engine
        │                 │
        └────────┬────────┘
                 ▼
             Tracking
                 │
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ▼         ▼         ▼
     Face       ANPR     Database
   Recognition           PostgreSQL
       │                   │
       └─────────┬─────────┘
                 ▼
              Alerts
                 │
              Telegram

Your existing design already has RTSP capture and motion detection in C, so those portions are good starting points.

So yes — we can convert the project to 100% C, but I recommend doing it module by module, not trying to rewrite everything at once.

The first step should be to convert the Python detector.py main detection engine into detector.c + main.c, then move to face recognition, ANPR, database, Telegram, and finally convert the C++ tracking/NPU components to C.

If you want, I can take this project and 
build the complete C-only project structure + source files + Makefile, starting from main.c and detector.c.


